1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now it's to you Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Survival tip, if you're going to make a fire using two sticks, always be sure one of them's a match.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Hey, this is broadcast number 55.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Hey, welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast, this is Clark Green.

5
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Mike wrote in this week, he said I continue to enjoy your podcast.

6
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
I usually listen to it the week it comes out.

7
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:07,000
I know a lot of people are catching up on the archives and you can find the podcast archives at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

8
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Joe, who's an assistant Scoutmaster in Santa Clara, California, wrote in to say thanks for your podcast.

9
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,000
They've been very informative.

10
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,000
I enjoyed listening to the latest as I take my dogs for walks in the morning.

11
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
I hope it's not disturbing the dogs too much, Joe.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
I imagine you're using earphones so they can't actually hear what goes on on the podcast.

13
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Our last podcast, Podcast 54, we had an interview with outdoor guide and writer Cliff Jacobson.

14
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,000
And Joe wrote in about that.

15
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
He says that was a great podcast.

16
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
And I must say that on the very next camp out, I will be putting my ground cloth inside my tent.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Then I will wait and watch all the Scouts looking at me with the old head sideways dog looking puzzled look on their faces.

18
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Well, let me give you a shorthand for that, Joe.

19
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
That's looking at it like a dog with a new pan.

20
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Yeah.

21
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
So now you've got that to work with, right?

22
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:10,000
I might, if asked, explain what I am doing and hopefully not appear to be as crazy as they think I am.

23
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Thank you for all the time you put into your podcast.

24
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
They are, as always, informative and entertaining.

25
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Well, thank you, Joe. Thanks for getting in touch.

26
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Not everyone was thrilled with the ground cloth inside the tent idea.

27
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,000
You know, Cliff's pretty passionate about that.

28
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
I'm pretty juiced about it myself.

29
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,000
I mean, that's what I do.

30
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
It's not a rule, but it has some good reasons behind it.

31
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Larry Geiger from Florida differed with me on this one.

32
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,000
First of all, he said, good program.

33
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,000
I've read Rustrum's books and I've learned quite a bit from his canoeing instruction.

34
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,000
That's an author that Cliff mentioned in the interview last week, Calvin Rutstrom.

35
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
I will step into it about tents, Larry goes on to say.

36
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
I camp almost exclusively in Florida.

37
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Cliff is wrong.

38
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Uh-oh.

39
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Number one, tent floors are for keeping things out.

40
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:10,000
When I zip up the door of my tent, I create a barrier against almost everything I want to be protected from.

41
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Rattlesnakes, water moccasins, coral snakes, copperhead snakes, mosquitoes, ants, lots and lots of ants.

42
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Scorpions, yes, there are scorpions in Florida.

43
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Wasps, deer flies, ticks, cicada killers, noceums, and spiders.

44
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Yeah, you know, I think you got something there, Larry.

45
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Yeah, you definitely want to keep stuff out of the tent.

46
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
And the floor does do that.

47
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
I use a heavy plastic ground cloth under my tent floor.

48
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Ants will chew right through a common tent floor.

49
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Well, you've got some crazy kind of ants down there in Florida.

50
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
And then number three, he says this.

51
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:51,000
What keeps you dry is a tent fly that overhangs the tent all the way around.

52
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Agreed.

53
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
My scouts don't get wet.

54
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,000
It does rain here in Florida unless they don't set their tent up correctly.

55
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Well, I think you got some points there, Larry.

56
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:08,000
I'm not going to say who's right and who's wrong because I don't think it's a right and a wrong thing.

57
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
And then I got this message from another Joe.

58
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Great show as always and a funny interview with Cliff.

59
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
I'll throw myself into the fray.

60
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
The tarp goes under the tent.

61
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
I spend most of my camping nights in Florida as well and I agree with Larry.

62
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Our troop is just a few miles up the road from Larry's.

63
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Perhaps the ground cloth in or under argument really depends on where you are camping.

64
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Hey, sounds good to me.

65
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
I've never camped in Florida, so I couldn't tell you.

66
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,000
I couldn't tell you, but I'll trust your experience and wisdom there, guys.

67
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:46,000
In this week's podcast, in Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less,

68
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:53,000
we're going to talk a little bit about an answer to Scouting Magazine's What Would You Do column.

69
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
I also have an email question to answer and a little story that I received from a listener

70
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
that comes under the title.

71
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,000
This has got to be true because there's just no way anybody would make this up.

72
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
So that's a lot to do. Let's get started, shall we?

73
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

74
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Hey, if you're a Scout leader, you're probably subscribed to Scouting Magazine.

75
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
And if you read Scouting Magazine,

76
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,000
one of my favorite features in there is What Would You Do,

77
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,000
the column What Would You Do where people write in with questions

78
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,000
and then other leaders write in with their answers.

79
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:47,000
The latest question that was posted online comes from not an adult leader,

80
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,000
but from a Scout, and it's about commanding respect.

81
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
And the question goes like this.

82
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Says, our troop has just seven Scouts who are 14 years of age or older, including me,

83
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,000
and then 15 to 20 younger Scouts.

84
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
On campouts, the younger Scouts don't listen to us,

85
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:11,000
and they give us attitude when we ask them to do stuff.

86
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
We have tried many things, but nothing seems to work.

87
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Could you give us some ideas?

88
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,000
And that's from Scout AS in San Jose, California.

89
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,000
This is the answer that I sent in.

90
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
So what leaders do you respect and why do you respect them?

91
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Is it because they commanded that you respect them?

92
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Probably not.

93
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,000
Baden-Powell said, I have frequently stated that anyone can be a commander,

94
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
and a trained man may often make an instructor,

95
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
but a leader is more like a poet.

96
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Now Baden-Powell speaks of three different sorts of people there,

97
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
the commander, the instructor, and the leader.

98
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,000
And that, to me, is an echo of the leaders described in a pretty well-known quote

99
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,000
by the Chinese philosopher Lao Tse.

100
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,000
And that quote is,

101
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:02,000
The best leader is the one who people are barely aware of.

102
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:06,000
The next best is one who is loved and praised by the people.

103
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
Next comes one who is feared.

104
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
Worst is one who is despised.

105
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:16,000
If the leader does not have enough faith in his people,

106
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
they will not have faith in him.

107
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:24,000
The best leader puts great value in words and says little,

108
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,000
so that when the work is finished, the people all say,

109
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,000
we did it ourselves.

110
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:37,000
Leadership that commands respect empowers people to do things for themselves.

111
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:41,000
The commander and instructor that Baden-Powell mentioned call attention to themselves,

112
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
and that results in praise or fear,

113
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:48,000
or has everybody kind of laughing up their sleeves at them.

114
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
We've all seen those three types of leaders before.

115
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Most importantly, I think a leader has to have faith in the people that they're leading.

116
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
They need to see the world as they do.

117
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:05,000
They need to exercise some empathy and value their time as important as their own.

118
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:11,000
I mean, real leaders create an atmosphere of shared accomplishment.

119
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:16,000
Now, this same question also was posted on Facebook,

120
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
and it garnered these comments,

121
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:23,000
and I include them here because I think they're very good examples

122
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:27,000
of typical reactions to the situation described in the question

123
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:32,000
by people who really don't have a very good understanding of scouting.

124
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:36,000
One of the answers was, request that a parent stays on campouts.

125
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
The kids will listen then.

126
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:41,000
All right, well, that's a possible solution,

127
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
but if the youth leaders have to rely on adults to make things happen,

128
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:49,000
they aren't really doing much leading, are they?

129
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,000
Another answer was, have a punishment.

130
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
If they don't cooperate, then they don't get rank advancements

131
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:58,000
and they can't come on the next campout.

132
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
Yeah, you know, if they get punished,

133
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:05,000
if you punish the scouts who are giving you attitude and not listening,

134
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
you don't have to worry about the next campout.

135
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
They're not going to be interested.

136
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:14,000
Scouting is about positive reinforcement and encouragement,

137
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
and that's why, you know, scouting is different.

138
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:23,000
The third answer I saw that, you know, was a typical reaction

139
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
is separate them into smaller groups,

140
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
giving each group a difficult task to complete.

141
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Each group should not include buddies.

142
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:34,000
The group that is successful first wins a prize.

143
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,000
Well, let me parse that statement a little bit.

144
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:42,000
First of all, the smaller groups, that's what we call patrols.

145
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Second of all, you definitely want to have buddies together in a patrol.

146
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
That's why boys are in scouting.

147
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
They want to do cool stuff with their friends.

148
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:56,000
If you rely on prizes or punishments or adults,

149
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,000
you're always going to need them.

150
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:04,000
I think learning to actually be a leader is a much better option.

151
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:08,000
Now, there were some really good answers on Facebook, too.

152
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:09,000
One of them was this.

153
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
This is where scouting is different.

154
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
Boys lead boys in scouting.

155
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:16,000
In scouting, we teach servant leadership at all levels.

156
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:19,000
Respect can't be commanded. It has to be earned.

157
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:26,000
The patrol method is the plan for earning respect.

158
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:30,000
And then there was also this answer.

159
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
Boy scout troops are led by boys,

160
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:35,000
so adults can't tell boys what to do.

161
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:39,000
How can a 14-year-old punish a 10-year-old?

162
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
You know, it's a learning curve on each side

163
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:46,000
for the senior patrol leader and the patrol leader and younger scouts.

164
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:50,000
Now, some of my listeners and readers jumped in.

165
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:51,000
Brian wrote this.

166
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
He says, I'm a new scoutmaster,

167
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,000
and we haven't really had any respect issues,

168
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:00,000
but I have noticed a major change in boys when they become patrol leaders.

169
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:05,000
Respect and relationships don't just happen by being in the same room with someone.

170
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
They happen by learning together and depending on one another.

171
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
Walter added, lead from the front.

172
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
When you're cleaning the latrines at summer camp,

173
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:19,000
the leader had better be the one with the toilet brush.

174
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:24,000
Yeah, you don't just dictate to people.

175
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
You lead them.

176
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
You do what they're doing.

177
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
You work along with them.

178
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:34,000
Always be doing more and always be helping.

179
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
If you're sitting on a chair and ordering people around,

180
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:39,000
they'll resent you, not follow you.

181
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:42,000
Help scouts get their tents set up right,

182
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
show them how to clean pots, et cetera,

183
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
and you're sure to earn their respect.

184
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Larry wrote in from Florida again,

185
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:55,000
and he said, I'm headed out tomorrow evening on an eight-mile backpacking trip.

186
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:58,000
There'll be 18 scouts, four of them over 14.

187
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:04,000
They've spent several weeks preparing, packing, planning, being inspected.

188
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Friday night, several things will fail.

189
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
Someone's pack will be too heavy,

190
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
someone will have the wrong shoes, it might be raining,

191
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
and someone will forget their poncho.

192
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:16,000
I'm not going to deal with it because I'm the scout master.

193
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:20,000
The other adults aren't going to deal with it because I told them not to.

194
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,000
Those four youth leaders will deal with it.

195
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:28,000
Believe me, they're not going to get a lot of attitude from the younger scouts

196
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:33,000
because they're going to be helping those scouts solve their problems.

197
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:38,000
So what kind of problems are you solving for your younger scouts?

198
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
I've seen a lot of troops.

199
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:44,000
I have never seen a busy, active troop with an attitude problem.

200
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:47,000
Well said, Larry. Very well said.

201
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:51,000
This is a pretty common concern, a pretty common question.

202
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,000
Older scouts get attitude from younger scouts.

203
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:55,000
They're very unsure of themselves.

204
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:59,000
They don't know how to lead, so they default into a couple of models.

205
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:03,000
They either become the big boss, and they holler and carry on,

206
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:07,000
and they try and force the younger scouts to respect them,

207
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:10,000
or they just kind of give up and they say,

208
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:13,000
well, we can't really get anywhere with this anyway,

209
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:17,000
so we'll just put in a minimal effort,

210
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:21,000
or the adults all step in and they try and fix a problem.

211
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:26,000
Well, our job as scout leaders that is reflected in these wonderful answers

212
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:31,000
that I got from my listeners and readers is this,

213
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:35,000
to coach and mentor our youth leadership,

214
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:41,000
to learn how to earn respect by being a servant leader,

215
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:46,000
by getting in there and helping out, by leading by doing,

216
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:53,000
and by challenging our troops and patrols to solve complex difficulties

217
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:56,000
without being able to fall back on us

218
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:01,000
and thus begin to discover their own talents as leaders.

219
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:02,000
That's what really, really works.

220
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
That's what's worked for a century,

221
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:09,000
and that's what I would recommend to you as a way to address these concerns.

222
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:40,000
Email, that is, folks.

223
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:43,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

224
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Hey, before I get into this email,

225
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
let me tell you that there's going to be a new feature on the blog,

226
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
and I'll tell you about that when we close,

227
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:02,000
that allows you to make a telephone call,

228
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
leave a voice message, question, or comment

229
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
that I can then feature on the podcast.

230
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:08,000
Pretty cool, huh?

231
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,000
I'll talk about that in a moment.

232
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:14,000
But this email came in, and it says this,

233
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
love the podcast, keep them coming.

234
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:17,000
Well, thank you.

235
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:21,000
I've recently taken over the duties as advancement chair for our troop,

236
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
and I and some of the other adult leaders have noticed

237
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:26,000
that our younger scouts have not been advancing along the trail

238
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
of first class very rapidly.

239
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
We have five or six boys just about to finish

240
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,000
their second year in scouting and are still second class.

241
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:38,000
Any suggestions how to provide some structure, opportunity,

242
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:41,000
motivation, or expectations on rank advancement?

243
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:45,000
We usually have some troop meeting time for scout skills

244
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:49,000
and try to encourage that the boys complete their cooking requirements

245
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
on our monthly camp outs.

246
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
Is there a role in this for patrol leaders?

247
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
Also, some of the adult leaders are concerned,

248
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:58,000
and I tend to agree, about easy sign-off requirements

249
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
with a one-time demonstration of the skill

250
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
as opposed to lasting skill development.

251
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:07,000
How should we raise expectations for these skills?

252
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:11,000
Or should we relax because the boys are having fun?

253
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:16,000
Many thanks, and that comes from Tom in Portland, Oregon.

254
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
Well, Tom, thanks for the kind words,

255
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:21,000
and I do thank you for getting in touch.

256
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
The situation you describe is actually pretty common.

257
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
It sounds like my troop.

258
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
There are a couple of perennial concerns reflected

259
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
in your questions.

260
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:32,000
That's one is scouts not advancing,

261
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:36,000
and the other is the quality of their skills.

262
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
Scouts do best, in my experience,

263
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
when they advance at their own speed.

264
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
You know, if everything else is going well,

265
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
if the scouts are active and happy,

266
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
I wouldn't go looking for problems.

267
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
As to structure, opportunity, motivation,

268
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
let the scouts provide that for themselves.

269
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
I think we only need to stay available

270
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:55,000
and provide a little encouragement.

271
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
I would ask your patrol leader's council

272
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
how they think advancement is going.

273
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Do they think that they should spend more time on it?

274
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,000
Do they think the scouts are advancing too slowly?

275
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:09,000
If they see a problem, how are they going to fix it?

276
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:12,000
Now, you also note a pretty perennial concern

277
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:16,000
with one-time demonstration requirements.

278
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
Well, my answer there is always

279
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:22,000
to read the actual requirements very carefully.

280
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:26,000
Does it say to demonstrate the skill two times?

281
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:29,000
Does it say to demonstrate them three or four times?

282
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
You know, there's a lot to be learned

283
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
by carefully reading the requirements

284
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
for ranks and badges.

285
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
I've read a lot of old scout literature,

286
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
and I can tell you that these same concerns

287
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:42,000
have been on scout masters' minds for a century.

288
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
Advancement is a great part of the program,

289
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
but it's only a part of the program.

290
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,000
It's not the whole ball of wax.

291
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
So I say all that to simply say

292
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:55,000
that you have already answered your own question.

293
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
Relax. If the boys stay involved,

294
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
if they're active and engaged,

295
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
everything else is just icing on the cake.

296
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
If they do the things scouts do,

297
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
they will advance, and what could be better?

298
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,000
You know, Baden Powell did have something to say

299
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
about this, believe it or not,

300
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:14,000
and he talked about scouts being rewarded

301
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
not for a particular level of skill

302
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
or some standardized achievement,

303
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:23,000
but for the amount of effort that they put into it,

304
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:26,000
and I'll have a link on the blog post

305
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,000
that contains this podcast so that you can read that.

306
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:32,000
But thanks, Tom, for listening again,

307
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
and thanks for getting in touch,

308
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
and I hope that helps.

309
00:18:47,000 --> 00:19:08,000
This has to be the truth, folks,

310
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:14,000
because there is no way anyone could make this up.

311
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:22,000
It's late in the year of 1927,

312
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:26,000
and an 11-year-old boy has just become a boy scout,

313
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:30,000
and he learns that he's going to need some things

314
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
to go scouting,

315
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
and so he approaches his father

316
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
and asks for an increase in his allowance

317
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:39,000
so that he can go and purchase the things that he needs,

318
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
and his father says,

319
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:44,000
well, I will consider your request,

320
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:47,000
because I'd like to see it in writing, if you would.

321
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:50,000
So the boy gets a pen and a piece of paper,

322
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:54,000
and he sits down, and this is what he writes.

323
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
Chapter 1.

324
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:59,000
My recent allowance is 40 cents.

325
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
This I used for aeroplanes

326
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
and other playthings of childhood,

327
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
but now I am a scout,

328
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:08,000
and I put away my childish things.

329
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
Before, I would spend 20 cents

330
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:13,000
of my 40 cents allowance in five minutes.

331
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:16,000
50 pockets, and nothing to gain,

332
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:19,000
and 20 cents to lose.

333
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:22,000
When I am a scout, I have to buy canteens,

334
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:26,000
haversacks, blankets, searchlights,

335
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:29,000
ponchos, things that will last for years,

336
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
and I can always use it.

337
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:34,000
Well, I can't use a chocolate marshmallow sundae

338
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
with vanilla ice cream,

339
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:39,000
and so I put in my plea for a raise of 30 cents

340
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
for me to buy scout things

341
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:44,000
and pay my own way more around.

342
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
And then he signs his name,

343
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:53,000
John Fitzgerald Francis Kennedy.

344
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:57,000
That's right, our 35th president, JFK,

345
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:00,000
as a boy scout at 11 years old.

346
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:04,000
And that comes from a longtime listener, reader,

347
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:09,000
and commenter, Walter, who sent that in,

348
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
and thank you so much, Walter.

349
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
I hope everybody enjoyed that.

350
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
Well, thanks again for listening

351
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
to the Scoutmaster podcast,

352
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
and here's how you can keep in touch.

353
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
First, you can read the Scoutmaster blog

354
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:38,000
at scoutmaster.typepad.com,

355
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:41,000
and you can also subscribe to the Scoutmaster newsletter.

356
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
And that's a monthly digest

357
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
of the Scoutmaster blog and podcast

358
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
and some great scouting resources

359
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:48,000
around a monthly theme.

360
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:52,000
And in February, the theme is the history of scouting.

361
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
Every newsletter includes a giveaway

362
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
from one of our affiliate sponsors.

363
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
February's giveaway will be

364
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
a package of great water bottles

365
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
from the Nalgene company.

366
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
You want to get on the blog

367
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

368
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
Look in the right-hand column,

369
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
and you'll see how you can sign up

370
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:14,000
to receive the Scoutmaster newsletter by email.

371
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
You can subscribe to the Scoutmaster podcast

372
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
on iTunes, and when you do,

373
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:28,000
feel free to leave a comment or a review or a rating.

374
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:31,000
You can follow us at Scoutmaster blog on Facebook

375
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:35,000
and ScoutmasterCG on Twitter.

376
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:38,000
You can email me, Clark Green,

377
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
with your comments and questions

378
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:46,000
at ClarkGreen, C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N,

379
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
at gmail.com.

380
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:52,000
That's ClarkGreen at gmail.com.

381
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:58,000
Now, as I said before,

382
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
there's a new way for you to participate

383
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:03,000
in the Scoutmaster podcast.

384
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:07,000
That is to call in to leave a voicemail

385
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,000
that will be recorded,

386
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:11,000
and it can be a comment or a question,

387
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:15,000
and it may be featured in the next Scoutmaster podcast.

388
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:16,000
So you ready?

389
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:24,000
Here's the number, 484-734-0002.

390
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:30,000
484-734-0002.

391
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:31,000
When you call that number,

392
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:35,000
you'll hear a recorded greeting from me.

393
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
Then you go ahead and leave your voicemail.

394
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
Hey, that should be fun.

395
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:44,000
Now comes the time to remind you

396
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:47,000
that the Scoutmaster blog and the Scoutmaster podcast

397
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:51,000
are not official publications of the Boy Scouts of America,

398
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:54,000
nor are they endorsed or sponsored by the Boy Scouts of America.

399
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:57,000
Hey, it's just me talking into a microphone,

400
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
trying to lend a hand to Scout leaders

401
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:04,000
and perhaps have a little bit of fun along the way.

402
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:05,000
Before we leave you,

403
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:08,000
here's the founder of Scouting, Sir Robert Baden-Powell.

404
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:11,000
Good luck to you and good camping.

405
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:12,000
Thank you, sir.

406
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
Until next time.

407
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,000
Good night.

